Identify what the question tests: **question**. Move be before the subject in a question. Put not after be in a negative. Keep the -ing form in both.","Move are before “my friends” to make this present-continuous question.","Choose **Are**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**am** does not match the subject’s person/number or the construction’s tense here. Move are before “my friends” to make this present-continuous question.","B":"**is** does not match the subject’s person/number or the construction’s tense here. Move are before “my friends” to make this present-continuous question.","C":"This construction uses its existing **are** form, so **Does** introduces the wrong auxiliary. Move are before “my friends” to make this present-continuous question.","D":"Move are before “my friends” to make this present-continuous question."}}},{"id":"c2b555ee-7159-5ace-ae69-72a86e3f2ea6","topicSlug":"present-continuous","level":"A1","prompt":"My friends are ____ the bill at the counter at the moment. (pay)","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"pays"},{"id":"B","text":"paid"},{"id":"C","text":"paying"},{"id":"D","text":"pay"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Many verbs add -ing. Usually remove a final silent e; some short verbs double their last consonant. Learn spelling with the verb. Focus on **ing form** in this question.","explanation":"After are in the present continuous, use the -ing form paying.","keywords":[],"source":"seed","skill":"ing-form","contextKey":"verb-pay","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-continuous","pcc-esol"],"version":"foundations-2026-09-v1","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"fingerprint":"d110221865334abda8726d56140c9070c904d0da935bd050ed9dd94891371d60","teaching":{"steps":["Identify what the question tests: **ing form**. Many verbs add -ing. Usually remove a final silent e; some short verbs double their last consonant. Learn spelling with the verb.","After are in the present continuous, use the -ing form paying.","Choose **paying**. Check the completed expression against the subject, time and intended meaning in the prompt."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"**pays** is not the -ing form required after be in this continuous construction. The verb changes to **paying**.","B":"**paid** is not the -ing form required after be in this continuous construction. The verb changes to **paying**.","C":"After are in the present continuous, use the -ing form paying.","D":"**pay** is not the -ing form required after be in this continuous construction. The verb changes to **paying**."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["The present continuous puts you inside an activity as it unfolds. Its two essential parts are a form of be and an -ing verb. It can describe the immediate moment or a temporary activity around now, such as a course this month. The form of be agrees with the subject; the -ing form does not."],"rules":[{"title":"Actions in progress","explanation":"Use am, is or are plus an -ing form for an activity in progress now. The form of be agrees with the subject.","examples":["The dog is sleeping by the fire.","We are discussing the route.","We **are renovating** the kitchen this month."],"pattern":"Subject + **am / is / are + -ing**","paragraphs":["I am studying this month need not mean that I am holding a book at the exact moment of speaking. The activity belongs to the current temporary period. Compare a stable habit, I study every evening, with an ongoing activity, I am studying now."]},{"title":"Building the verb","explanation":"Many verbs add -ing. Usually remove a final silent e; some short verbs double their last consonant. Learn spelling with the verb.","examples":["write → writing","cut → cutting","She **isn’t using** the laptop right now."],"pattern":"make → mak**ing** · run → ru**nning** · lie → l**ying**","paragraphs":["A silent final e is usually dropped before -ing: make → making. Some short verbs double their final consonant: sit → sitting. Verbs ending in -ie change it to y: lie → lying. Spelling is partly lexical, so check unfamiliar verbs instead of adding letters blindly."]},{"title":"Questions and negatives","explanation":"Move be before the subject in a question. Put not after be in a negative. Keep the -ing form in both.","examples":["Are they loading the van?","I am not using the lift.","**Are** you **waiting** for someone?"],"pattern":"**Are** they **working**? / They **are not working**.","paragraphs":["Only be moves in the question. The -ing form remains after the subject: Is she reading? In the negative, insert not after be: She is not reading. A short answer keeps only the auxiliary: Yes, she is."]},{"title":"Contrasts and edge cases","explanation":"State verbs such as know and own do not normally use continuous aspect in their ordinary senses. A verb can change sense: I think you are right expresses an opinion, while I am thinking about the problem describes an activity. The continuous can also describe a future arrangement.","examples":["We **are renovating** the kitchen this month.","She **isn’t using** the laptop right now.","**Are** you **waiting** for someone?"]}],"recap":["Subject + **am / is / are + -ing**","make → mak**ing** · run → ru**nning** · lie → l**ying**","**Are** they **working**? / They **are not working**."]},"stage":"2 · Everyday descriptions","prerequisites":["imperatives"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
